WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.069 line:0%
-Chiwetel, nice to see you, bud.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.637 line:0%
-Thank you.
Nice to see you, too.

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:05.671 line:0%
Pleasure to be here.
-Thank you.

00:00:05.671 --> 00:00:07.573 line:0%
Thank you so much
for doing the show.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.910 line:0%
I know you made it
back to London,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.346 line:0%
but the quarantine started
for you here in New York,

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:14.814 line:0%
is that right?

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.483 line:0%
-That's right, that's right.
I was in New York

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:20.854 line:0%
when it all --
when it all kicked off,

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.521 line:0%
which was -- I mean,
it was pretty intense.

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:24.557 line:0%
It was intense for everybody,
of course, you know.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.959 line:0%
But it was pretty full-on
in New York.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:29.328 line:0%
And I ended up getting on

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:33.166 line:0%
what they called
a repatriation flight,

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:36.369 line:0%
you know, which sounded,
like, very Orwellian.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.772 line:0%
I was like the only person
in JFK.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.908 line:0%
-Wow.
-It was incredibly surreal.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:44.878 line:0%
Just pulling up just to the
terminal

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.446 line:0%
and being in the only car
in the whole thing,

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:50.650 line:0%
it was really -- it really
hit home at that point.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.587 line:0%
-Your family is -- Your mom
and dad are both from Nigeria?

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:55.789 line:0%
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:57.890 line:0%
-And your dad, I read somewhere,

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.694 line:0%
your dad was a singer, like,
a pretty famous musician, right?

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:02.895 line:0%
-Yeah, he was.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:06.198 line:0%
He was a pretty famous musician
in Nigeria.

00:01:06.198 --> 00:01:07.600 line:0%
He was a doctor, as well.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.705 line:0%
But he was pretty big in kind of
sort of African high-life music,

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:17.710 line:0%
with a lot of sort of jazz
and sort of funk influence.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.714 line:0%
And I, you know -- I mean,
it was, I mean, amazing for me

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:25.284 line:0%
growing up and starting to
learn, when we'd visit Nigeria,

00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:28.354 line:0%
just how well known
he was there.

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:33.359 line:0%
People just on the street would
break into his music, you know?

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:37.230 line:0%
-And how old were you
when you saw that?

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.698 line:0%
-I was like 7,
I guess, you know,

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:42.502 line:0%
7, 8 years old
when I was kind of aware

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.636 line:0%
that people were singing
my father's songs,

00:01:44.636 --> 00:01:47.973 line:0%
and it was just sort of
in random places in Nigeria.

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:50.142 line:0%
And, you know, people would
break into his music,

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:51.578 line:0%
and I would just be like

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:53.213 line:0%
sort of getting it
and piecing it together

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:56.315 line:0%
and realizing just
that sense of community

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:58.584 line:0%
and the influence
that he was having.

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:02.422 line:0%
-And your mom
was a backup singer?

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:04.757 line:0%
-Yeah, she was a backup singer
in his band.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:08.961 line:0%
So, you know, they were like --
And actually, his mom, as well,

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:11.030 line:0%
my grandmother
on my father's side

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.865 line:0%
was also one of the backup
singers in the band.

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:16.635 line:0%
So it was like
a little family affair.

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:19.405 line:0%
-Did you think of getting into
music or your sisters?

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.575 line:0%
-I did. I mean, I never had
my father's voice, sadly,

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.144 line:0%
but I loved --

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:28.447 line:0%
you know, he was --
his influence on me was just

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:30.649 line:0%
I think about
in sort of entertainment

00:02:30.649 --> 00:02:34.454 line:0%
and being able to kind of
communicate with people

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.223 line:0%
and how music
and for me then acting

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:40.627 line:0%
was also a way
of expressing self, you know,

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:44.897 line:0%
and, you know, thinking about
self-expression,

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.799 line:0%
and thinking about sort of
self-understanding

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.669 line:0%
and communicating that
with a group of people.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.904 line:0%
And that was the kind of --

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:54.807 line:0%
I think that was
the sort of line that I took

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:57.643 line:0%
that was sort of like his,
in a way.

00:02:57.643 --> 00:02:59.712 line:0%
-Yeah. That's wild
you got the bug right there.

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:00.813 line:0%
That's pretty wild.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:02.281 line:0%
Then look what you've done
with everything.

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:03.782 line:0%
I mean, you've just been
crushing it,

00:03:03.782 --> 00:03:06.085 line:0%
every single movie you do.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.687 line:0%
I mean, you're getting nominated
for Oscars.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.590 line:0%
It's like --
It's a pretty awesome career.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.360 line:0%
I want to talk about
"The Old Guard" on Netflix.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.596 line:0%
Big hit. Congrats on that.

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:18.531 line:0%
-Thank you.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:21.467 line:0%
-That's kind of a fun thing,
too, to have a movie on Netflix.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:25.271 line:0%
'cause everyone's home
right now, so --

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.906 line:0%
-Well, that is
a great advantage, you know,

00:03:27.906 --> 00:03:31.544 line:0%
that there is, like, a proper
captive audience for the movie.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.179 line:0%
It's amazing.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.148 line:0%
You were saying somewhere
that you think

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:38.051 line:0%
this is a big either
step forward in movies and film,

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:41.454 line:0%
or is that because of
the Netflix part?

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.289 line:0%
-Yeah, I mean, I think
it's all of it, you know?

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.959 line:0%
"The Old Guard" I think is
a great film in its own right.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.061 line:0%
And I think it's been
brilliantly directed

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:51.263 line:0%
by Gina Prince-Bythewood.

00:03:51.263 --> 00:03:56.401 line:0%
But also, it encapsulates,
I think, this idea of how film

00:03:56.401 --> 00:04:00.539 line:0%
and how media in broad terms
can really be inclusive

00:04:00.539 --> 00:04:03.910 line:0%
and how that is part of the --
With this film,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.379 line:0%
it's sort of an integral part
of the narrative

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.681 line:0%
is this kind of sense
of inclusivity.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:11.117 line:0%
And everybody is represented
in the film.

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:12.651 line:0%
All sorts of different groups,

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:14.053 line:0%
people of different
walks of life

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.188 line:0%
are all represented
in their own way.

00:04:16.188 --> 00:04:20.526 line:0%
And that's both in the story and
also behind the camera, as well.

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:23.729 line:0%
And I think that that is
a really important marker.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.998 line:0%
And just how popular the film
has been, as well,

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:29.535 line:0%
is just an important marker
for the way these things can go.

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.871 line:0%
I think that that is
what's really going to be

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:33.572 line:0%
a saving grace of our industry

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:35.775 line:0%
and an important part
of our industry forward

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.643 line:0%
is really representing
everybody.

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:38.644 line:0%
-Yeah. I agree.

00:04:38.644 --> 00:04:40.312 line:0%
Can you set up the movie?

00:04:40.312 --> 00:04:42.314 line:0%
-So, "The Old Guard" is about

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:46.418 line:0%
a group of mercenaries
led by Charlize Theron

00:04:46.418 --> 00:04:49.321 line:0%
who also happen to be immortal.

00:04:49.321 --> 00:04:50.589 line:0%
I mean, stop right there.

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.759 line:0%
-I know, exactly.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:55.494 line:0%
You guys -- I mean, seriously,
she's amazing, unbelievable.

00:04:55.494 --> 00:04:57.930 line:0%
You guys together --
The film is great,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.600 line:0%
but she is just brilliant.
Oh, my gosh.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.436 line:0%
-Yeah. They kind of run into

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:08.607 line:0%
a group of nefarious characters
who are not immortal,

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:13.346 line:0%
but they are very sort of
ethically and morally ambiguous.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:16.048 line:0%
I would categorize myself
as one of that gang.

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:19.418 line:0%
And then sort of chaos ensues.

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:22.955 line:0%
-Yeah. It's very good.
I want to show a clip.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:26.759 line:0%
Here's Chiwetel Ejiofor
in "The Old Guard." Take a look.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.694 line:0%
[ Indistinct conversations ]

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.864 line:0%
-Last overflight confirmed
the personnel on-site.

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:34.433 line:0%
♪♪

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:37.069 line:0%
No food or water
being brought in.

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:42.174 line:0%
♪♪

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:44.410 line:0%
-That means
they're moving them soon.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.413 line:0%
-And when they do, most likely,
they'll be separated,

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:50.983 line:0%
and odds are,
you'll never find them again.

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:53.619 line:0%
Has to be done quickly,
by the best.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.021 line:0%
And your team's
the best I've ever seen.

00:05:56.021 --> 00:05:58.290 line:0%
You can name your price.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:05.130 line:0%
♪♪

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.400 line:0%
-We'll invoice you
when it's done.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.870 line:0%
-My thanks to Chiwetel Ejiofor.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.172 line:0%
"The Old Guard"
is available now on Netflix.

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:15.541 line:0%
Stay safe, buddy.
Give my love to London, okay?

